%I A118674 #31 Nov 08 2026 22:57:45
%S A118674 0,9,60,93,140,429,620,893,2576,3689,5280,15089,21576,30849,88020,
%T A118674 125829,179876,513093,733460,1048469,2990600,4274993,6111000,17430569,
%U A118674 24916560,35617593,101592876,145224429,207594620,592126749,846430076,1209950189,3451167680,4933356089
%N A118674 Nonnegative values x of solutions (x, y) to the Diophantine equation x^2 + (x + 31)^2 = y^2.
%C A118674 Also values x of Pythagorean triples (x, x+31, y).
%C A118674 Corresponding values y of solutions (x, y) are in A157646.
%C A118674 For the generic case x^2 + (x + p)^2 = y^2 with p = 2*m^2 - 1 a (prime) number in A066436 see A118673 or A129836.
%C A118674 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/a(n-3) = 3 + 2*sqrt(2).
%C A118674 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/a(n-1) = (33 + 8*sqrt(2))/31 for n mod 3 = {1, 2}.
%C A118674 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/a(n-1) = (1539 + 850*sqrt(2))/31^2 for n mod 3 = 0.
%C A118674 The nonnegative solutions of x^2 + (x+31)^2 = y^2 form exactly three Pell orbits with canonical seeds (x,y) = (0,31), (9,41), and (60,109). In the notation of the cited Pell-orbit catalog, H(31) = 3 and P(31) = 2: three solution streams are needed to obtain the increasing x-sequence, and two of these streams consist of primitive Pythagorean triples. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 02 2026
%H A118674 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A118674/b118674.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A118674 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23592032">A Unified Pell-Orbit Theory for the Diophantine Equation x^2+(x+d)^2=y^2: Complete Parametrization, Recurrences, and an OEIS Catalogue</a>, Zenodo (2026), Section 11.4.5.
%H A118674 <a href="/index/Rec#order_07">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1).
%F A118674 a(n) = 6*a(n-3) - a(n-6) + 62 for n > 6; a(1)=0, a(2)=9, a(3)=60, a(4)=93, a(5)=140, a(6)=429.
%F A118674 G.f.: x^2*(9 + 51*x + 33*x^2 - 7*x^3 - 17*x^4 - 7*x^5)/((1-x)*(1 - 6*x^3 + x^6)).
%F A118674 a(3*k + 1) = 31*A001652(k) for k >= 0.
%e A118674 The three canonical seeds give the triples (0,31,31), (9,40,41), and (60,91,109). Applying the Pell transformation (x,y) -> (3*x + 2*y + 31, 4*x + 3*y + 62) gives the next triples (93,124,155), (140,171,221), and (429,460,629), respectively. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 02 2026
%t A118674 ClearAll[a]; Evaluate[Array[a, 6]] = {0, 9, 60, 93, 140, 429}; a[n_] := a[n] = 6*a[n-3] - a[n-6] + 62; Table[a[n], {n, 1, 31}] (* _Jean-François Alcover_, Dec 27 2011, after given formula *)
%t A118674 LinearRecurrence[{1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1}, {0,9,60,93,140,429,620}, 50] (* _G. C. Greubel_, Mar 31 2018 *)
%o A118674 (PARI) {forstep(n=0, 850000000, [1, 3], if(issquare(2*n^2+62*n+961), print1(n, ",")))};
%o A118674 (Magma) I:=[0,9,60,93,140,429,620]; [n le 7 select I[n] else Self(n-1) - 6*Self(n-3) - 6*Self(n-4) - Self(n-6) + Self(n-7): n in [1..50]]; // _G. C. Greubel_, Mar 31 2018
%o A118674 (Python)
%o A118674 def a118674(n):
%o A118674     if n < 1:
%o A118674         raise ValueError("n must be >= 1")
%o A118674     streams = [(0, 31), (9, 41), (60, 109)]
%o A118674     for _ in range(n - 1):
%o A118674         i = min(range(3), key=lambda j: streams[j][0])
%o A118674         x, y = streams[i]
%o A118674         streams[i] = (3*x + 2*y + 31, 4*x + 3*y + 62)
%o A118674     return min(x for x, y in streams)
%o A118674 print([a118674(n) for n in range(1, 41)])
%o A118674 # _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 02 2026
%Y A118674 Cf. A157646, A066436 (primes of the form 2*n^2-1), A118673, A129836, A001652, A002193 (decimal expansion of sqrt(2)), A156035 (decimal expansion of 3 + 2*sqrt(2)), A157647 (decimal expansion of (33 + 8*sqrt(2))/31), A157648 (decimal expansion of (1539 + 850*sqrt(2))/31^2).
